WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:07.549
Speaker 1: Major legal news Out of the state
of Georgia, a grand jury has been approved

00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:12.280
to investigate Donald Trump's election interference.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.460
Remember those 11000 votes he wanted?

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.560
Speaker 2: I need 11000 votes.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:18.560
Give me a break.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:19.560
Yeah.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.460
Speaker 1: It turns out crimes may have been
committed there, and we may soon see the difference

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:30.820
in power between a congressional subpoena
and your regular good old fashioned district

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.790
court grand jury subpoena.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:41.480
CNN Reporting Day four Atlanta area granted
special grand jury to probe Trump's election

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.010
interference.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:47.260
This is not good news for the former president,
the article reads.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:51.340
An Atlanta area district attorney investigating
former President Donald Trump's efforts to

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:56.710
overturn the 2020 results in Georgia will
be allowed to seat a special grand jury this

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.120
spring.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.140
Fulton County D.A.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:05.220
Fanny Willis requested last week to seat a
special grand jury starting May 2nd.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.399
Some are saying that's an ambitious date,
but we'll see.

00:01:08.399 --> 00:01:12.369
Fulton County Superior Court judges approved
the request on Monday, though the special

00:01:12.369 --> 00:01:16.100
grand jury does not have the authority to
issue an indictment.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:21.590
The move will allow Willis to seat a panel
exclusively focused on gathering evidence

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:22.869
in the Trump investigation.

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:28.130
She said she needed such a grand jury in order
to issue subpoenas to compel witnesses to

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:33.829
testify and gather evidence a step toward
pursuing possible criminal charges.

00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:38.840
Understand this is all under the umbrella
of criminality.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.539
Willis said she expects to decide whether
to bring charges against Trump in the first

00:01:43.539 --> 00:01:49.310
half of 2022, which gives us that that debt
self-imposed deadline is June 30th.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.389
Now can you do that between May 2nd and June
30th?

00:01:52.389 --> 00:01:54.299
You can legal analyzes.

00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:55.729
I've read say you can, but it is.

00:01:55.729 --> 00:01:57.380
It is certainly aggressive.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:02.899
Willis wrote last week that her office received
information indicating a reasonable probability

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:07.530
that the state of Georgia's administration
of elections in 2020, including the state's

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:13.910
election of president of the U.S., was subject
to possible criminal disruptions.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:18.290
This is very, very real and very, very serious.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.000
This is going to give the D.A.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.360
Fanny Willis the power to file criminal subpoenas.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:30.200
These are much more difficult to delay or
to avoid than the congressional subpoenas

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:35.090
that we've been seeing under the umbrella
of the Trump riot investigation.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:44.010
As far as she believes, she has enough materials
to suggest crimes were committed and in theory,

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.920
at least depending on if and what Donald Trump
comes up with.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:56.159
If the situation surfaces, she could compel
testimony to the grand jury to further the

00:02:56.159 --> 00:02:58.379
belief that criminality took place.

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:02.840
Now, grand juries in Georgia have some interesting
rules.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:09.189
The accused in Georgia does not have a right
to appear before a grand jury.

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:14.209
They don't have a right to have their attorney
appear before a grand jury.

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:17.830
They don't have the right to present witnesses
or cross-examine witnesses.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:23.640
If you are accused in a grand jury is investigating
you in Georgia, you are not.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:28.970
It is not a criminal trial with all of the
rights that come as a defendant in a criminal

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:30.790
trial.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:39.560
You can testify or refuse to testify in accordance
with a Fifth Amendment invocation, and the

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:43.750
U.S. judicial system considers lying to a
grand jury.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:46.370
Very, very serious.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:05:01.240
And this is very quickly having the potential
to become a serious criminal matter 

00:05:01.240 --> 00:06:38.440
for Donald Trump.

